  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will continue to dominate through the early to\nmiddle part of next week. A weak, dry cold front has pushed\nthrough the area early this morning. Clouds are now decreasing\nand there could be some very patchy valley fog. This should \nallow for slightly cooler temperatures today followed by a \nwarming trend this weekend. We will see some cold temperatures\ntonight and again Saturday night, with overnight lows in the\nmid-20s to low 30s both nights. There will be areas of river\nvalley fog each night/early morning as well. During the day it \nwill warm up into the upper 50s to mid 60s with plenty of \nsunshine both Saturday and Sunday.\n\nThe other issue to take into account is a typical model high \nbias of afternoon dewpoints in this pattern, therefore blending\nin guidance to lower these afternoon dew points and humidity\nlevels through the weekend. \n\nYet another cold front attempts to break down the ridge a bit \nearly next week but also looks moisture starved. The pattern overall \nthrough most of next week remains dry or with brief passing showers \npossible later in the week. Temperatures will be warmest on\nMonday, well into the 60s. After the dry cold front presses\nthrough it's back down into the 50s for Tuesday, with cold\novernight lows in the mid-20s to low 30s Tuesday night.",
